WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.694
Embedded firmware often has multiple
execution contexts: a main loop,

2
00:00:03.774 --> 00:00:07.661
interrupt handlers, timers, DMA engines,
RTOS tasks, and sometimes async executors.

3
00:00:07.741 --> 00:00:11.201
These contexts can interact through shared
state, hardware flags, buffers,

4
00:00:11.281 --> 00:00:12.719
queues, and peripheral events.

5
00:00:12.799 --> 00:00:15.194
Rust helps with ownership and data-race
prevention,

6
00:00:15.274 --> 00:00:17.723
but the concurrency design still needs to
be explicit.

7
00:00:17.803 --> 00:00:21.157
Shared mutable state should be protected
through an appropriate pattern.

8
00:00:21.237 --> 00:00:24.591
Depending on the platform and framework,
that may mean critical sections,

9
00:00:24.671 --> 00:00:27.173
atomics, locks, message passing, ownership
transfer,

10
00:00:27.253 --> 00:00:29.170
or framework-supported resource models.

11
00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:32.710
The key is that the code should show who
may read or mutate a value and under what

12
00:00:32.790 --> 00:00:33.376
conditions.

13
00:00:33.456 --> 00:00:37.076
DMA introduces special ownership questions
because hardware may read or write a

14
00:00:37.156 --> 00:00:39.020
buffer while software also wants access.

15
00:00:39.100 --> 00:00:41.708
Defensive code defines who owns the buffer
at each moment,

16
00:00:41.788 --> 00:00:44.344
when software may inspect it, when
hardware may write it,

17
00:00:44.424 --> 00:00:48.097
how completion is signaled, and what
happens on cancellation, timeout, or error.

18
00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:49.508
Timing and signaling matter.

19
00:00:49.588 --> 00:00:52.729
A design may be memory-safe and still have
races in state transitions,

20
00:00:52.809 --> 00:00:56.483
missed signals, unexpected latency, or
cleanup that only works on the happy path.

21
00:00:56.563 --> 00:00:59.544
Reviewers should ask whether interrupt
handlers do minimal work,

22
00:00:59.624 --> 00:01:03.350
whether shared flags are used correctly,
and whether long operations are bounded.

23
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:06.412
The defensive goal is concurrency that is
bounded and reviewable.

24
00:01:06.492 --> 00:01:09.420
The firmware should make shared-state
rules, buffer ownership,

25
00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:13.173
interrupt behavior, and completion paths
clear enough that a reviewer can reason

26
00:01:13.253 --> 00:01:16.980
about normal execution, overload,
cancellation, and recovery without guessing.
